`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'schema-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: '#schema' },
				{ id: 'no-blanks', kind: 'text', selector: '#schema', matches: '^[^…]*$' },
				{ id: 'entities', kind: 'count', selector: '#schema tr.entity', min: 4 },
				{ id: 'keys', kind: 'count', selector: '#schema td.key', min: 4 },
				{ id: 'key-is-id', kind: 'text', selector: '#schema td.key', matches: '\\b(?:id|ID)\\b|identifier|number' },
				{ id: 'request-separate', kind: 'text', selector: '#schema tr.entity.request td.fields', matches: 'phone|name|e-?mail|contact' },
				{ id: 'relations', kind: 'text', selector: '#schema td.rel', matches: '_id|refer|belong|link|relat|→' },
				{ id: 'pd-marked', kind: 'text', selector: '#schema tr.entity.request td.pd', matches: '^\\s*[Yy]es' }
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Two editors open the same JSON file with a booking counter at the same time. Both read 10 and both write 9. What happened?',
				options: [
					'The file knows nothing about two authors, so one edit was lost: it should have become 8',
					'The file added both edits together and wrote 8',
					'The file gave the second editor an error'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A file keeps only the last write, and doesn’t track who made it or from which value. This is a “lost update”.',
					'No: a file can’t combine edits, it accepts whatever was written last.',
					'No: a file doesn’t know anyone else has it open, so there is no error. There is no second edit either.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each term to what it means',
				pairs: [
					['Table', 'a set of records of one kind: for example, all services'],
					['Row', 'one record: one service with all its values'],
					['Column', 'one field of every record: for example, the price, with a set type'],
					['Primary key', 'a field that names a row unambiguously and never repeats'],
					['Foreign key', 'a field that points to a row of another table']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What does a database do for you that a plain file doesn’t?',
				options: [
					'Makes sure two simultaneous edits can’t overwrite each other',
					'Refuses a link to a row that doesn’t exist',
					'Finds the records you need quickly, without reading everything',
					'Works out for itself what data your site needs'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: locks and transactions do this.',
					'Yes: a foreign key rejects the write if there is nothing to point to.',
					'Yes: indexes do this, and lesson 4 of this part covers them.',
					'No: what to store is your decision. That is why you need a data schema.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What is the best choice for the primary key of a table of services?',
				options: ['An automatic number (id) that never changes', 'The name of the service', 'The price of the service'],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A number means nothing by itself, which is why it never changes: a name can be corrected while every link to the row stays intact.',
					'No: a name can be changed, and two services can share one. A key must be unique and permanent.',
					'No: prices repeat and change.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A visitor sent a request. Where should it be stored?',
				options: [
					'In a separate table of requests, with a link to the service',
					'In a “requests” field inside the service’s row',
					'In the service’s name, separated by commas'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. One service has many requests, each with its own date and contact details, and they are also personal data, which is kept and deleted under its own rules.',
					'No: one field can’t hold many requests with dates and contacts, and you couldn’t edit one of them on its own.',
					'No: that mixes the data together, and you would have to search it by eye.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'order',
				prompt: 'Put the steps of designing a data schema in order',
				items: [
					'Pull the nouns out of the brief: services, people, proofs, requests',
					'Decide which of them are entities of their own, with many instances',
					'Give each one a key (id)',
					'Describe the fields and their types',
					'Draw the links between the entities',
					'Mark the fields that hold personal data'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'data-db-what',
				front: 'What is a database, and why is it better than a file?',
				back: 'A store plus a guard program (the DBMS). It stops two edits from overwriting each other, keeps links consistent, searches quickly and hands out permissions.'
			},
			{
				id: 'data-db-lost-update',
				front: 'What is a “lost update”?',
				back: 'Two people read the old value and each write their own: the last write overwrites the first, and nobody gets an error. A database doesn’t allow this.'
			},
			{
				id: 'data-db-table-row-col',
				front: 'Table, row and column: what are they?',
				back: 'A table holds records of one kind. A row is one record. A column is one field of every record, with a set type.'
			},
			{
				id: 'data-db-keys',
				front: 'Primary key and foreign key',
				back: 'A primary key (usually id) names a row unambiguously and never repeats. A foreign key is a field that points to the primary key of another table.'
			},
			{
				id: 'data-db-entities',
				front: 'How do you find a project’s entities?',
				back: 'Pull the nouns out of the brief, keep those with many instances (service, person, request), and give each a key, fields and links. A request is a separate entity.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{
				title: 'PostgreSQL documentation: Tutorial',
				url: 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html',
				licence: 'proprietary-link',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{ title: 'SQLite: appropriate uses for SQLite', url: 'https://www.sqlite.org/whentouse.html', licence: 'PD', kind: 'docs', lang: 'en' },
			{
				title: 'Relational model (Wikipedia)',
				url: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relational_model',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'article',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'PostgreSQL: Concurrency control, introduction',
				url: 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html',
				licence: 'proprietary-link',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		videos: [],
		guide: {
			intro: 'We’re designing your site’s memory. You don’t have to build a database yet: first you need a schema, which is a list of the things the site has to remember and the links between them.',
			hints: [
				'The model is in the first row: an entity, a key, fields separated by commas, a link and a note on personal data. Repeat that order in the other rows.',
				'A request is what a visitor sends through the form. Its fields are the visitor’s contact details and a date. Its link goes to the service it was sent about.',
				'Personal data is anything that can identify a living person: a visitor’s name and phone number certainly do. Start the request row’s note with “yes”.'
			],
			mistakes: {
				'schema-exists': 'The schema block is gone. It was in the page, so don’t delete it.',
				'no-blanks': 'There is still an ellipsis “…” somewhere. Replace it with your own text.',
				entities: 'You need at least four rows with the class entity: services, people, proofs and requests.',
				keys: 'Every entity needs a td.key cell with its key.',
				'key-is-id': 'Write id in the key: that is the usual name for a row’s unique number.',
				'request-separate': 'The request’s fields don’t show the visitor’s contact details. Write what exactly they leave: a name, a phone number, an email.',
				relations: 'There are no links. Say what each entity is linked to: for example “refers to a service (item_id)”.',
				'pd-marked': 'A visitor’s name and phone number are personal data. In the request row, start the cell with “yes”.'
			},
			outro: 'Now your project has a schema. In the next lesson we’ll turn it into tables and learn to ask them questions in SQL.'
		},
		say: {
			SQL: 'S Q L',
			JSON: 'jay son',
			id: 'eye dee',
			DBMS: 'D B M S',
			SQLite: 'S Q L light',
			PostgreSQL: 'post gress Q L',
			Excel: 'excel',
			CSV: 'C S V',
			COMMIT: 'commit'
		}
	};
</script>

